        },
        {
          "text": "y = A cos 2x + B sin 2x",
          "synonyms": [
            "the general solution for purely imaginary roots"
          ],
          "mustInclude": true
        },
        {
          "text": "A = 1 from y(0) = 1",
          "synonyms": [
            "the cosine coefficient"
          ],
          "mustInclude": true
        },
        {
          "text": "B = 3 from y'(0) = 6",
          "synonyms": [
            "differentiate to get 2B = 6"
          ],
          "mustInclude": true
        }
      ],
      "mcq": {
        "correct": "$y = \\cos 2x + 3\\sin 2x$",
        "distractors": [
          "$y = \\cos 2x + 6\\sin 2x$",
          "$y = \\cos 2x + 3\\sin x$",
          "$y = 3\\cos 2x + \\sin 2x$"
        ]
      },
      "explanation": "$m^2 + 4 = 0$ gives $m = \\pm 2i$, so $y = A\\cos 2x + B\\sin 2x$. Then $y(0) = A = 1$, and $\\frac{dy}{dx} = -2A\\sin 2x + 2B\\cos 2x$ gives $\\frac{dy}{dx}(0) = 2B = 6$, so $B = 3$. The factor 2 in the derivative is what makes the answer $3\\sin 2x$ rather than $6\\sin 2x$ - the most common error with a trigonometric complementary function.",
      "reviewStatus": "AI_UNREVIEWED",
