    {
      "id": "0012",
      "front": "A cup of coffee in a room at $20^\\circ$C obeys $\\frac{dT}{dt} = -k(T - 20)$. It is $100^\\circ$C initially and $60^\\circ$C after 10 minutes. Find its temperature after 20 minutes.",
      "back": "$40^\\circ$C",
      "answerType": "NUMERIC",
      "keyPoints": [
        {
          "text": "T - 20 = 80 e^(-kt)",
          "synonyms": [
            "solve the differential equation"
          ],
          "mustInclude": true
        },
        {
          "text": "e^(-10k) = 1/2 from the 10 minute reading",
          "synonyms": [
            "40 = 80 e^(-10k)"
          ],
          "mustInclude": true
        },
        {
          "text": "after 20 minutes T - 20 = 80/4 = 20",
          "synonyms": [
            "square the half-life factor"
          ],
          "mustInclude": true
        },
        {
          "text": "40",
          "mustInclude": true
        }
      ],
      "mcq": {
        "correct": "$40^\\circ$C",
        "distractors": [
          "$20^\\circ$C",
          "$50^\\circ$C",
          "$30^\\circ$C"
        ]
      },
      "explanation": "Solving gives $T - 20 = 80e^{-kt}$. At $t = 10$, $40 = 80e^{-10k}$, so $e^{-10k} = \\frac{1}{2}$. At $t = 20$, $e^{-20k} = \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^2 = \\frac{1}{4}$, so $T - 20 = 20$ and $T = 40$. The excess temperature halves every 10 minutes; the answer $50^\\circ$C comes from assuming the temperature itself, rather than the excess over the room, falls by a constant amount each 10 minutes.",
